1
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:03,702
Good morning, listeners.

2
00:00:03,652 --> 00:00:07,525
Today is October 26, 2026.

3
00:00:07,525 --> 00:00:15,258
Overnight, the S and P, Nasdaq, and Dow steadied as Treasury yields and Fed rate expectations took focus.

4
00:00:15,258 --> 00:00:24,081
Wall Street now enters a five-day super stress-test where central banks, inflation data, mega-cap earnings, and geopolitics collide.

5
00:00:24,081 --> 00:00:32,292
Middle East conflict is driving crude oil volatility, shifting inflation expectations and reshaping the Federal Reserve rate path.

6
00:00:32,292 --> 00:00:40,197
Those pressures feed straight into US Treasury yields, the ultimate pricing anchor for equity valuations and sector rotation.

7
00:00:40,197 --> 00:00:46,974
Asset repricing this week will depend on expectation gaps against consensus rather than headline numbers alone.

8
00:00:46,974 --> 00:00:52,917
Wednesday brings the Federal Reserve rate decision, the centerpiece of this global central bank week.

9
00:00:52,917 --> 00:00:59,878
Markets firmly expect rates to remain on hold, with no new quarterly economic projections released at this meeting.

10
00:00:59,878 --> 00:01:07,084
Traders are laser-focused on statement wording changes and Chair Powell's press conference clues for the December policy path.

11
00:01:07,084 --> 00:01:13,199
A hawkish tilt will push Treasury yields higher and directly pressure high-valuation growth stocks.

12
00:01:13,199 --> 00:01:19,657
A dovish signal preaching patience on incoming data would open a welcome relief window for risk assets.

13
00:01:19,657 --> 00:01:28,665
Approaching US midterm elections also make oil prices and inflation politically sensitive, complicating monetary policy and military timing.

14
00:01:28,665 --> 00:01:36,606
Non-US central banks are taking the stage simultaneously, amplifying global liquidity swings through diverging policy paths.

15
00:01:36,606 --> 00:01:42,942
The Bank of Canada decides right after the Fed amid deep market division over its future rate trajectory.

16
00:01:42,942 --> 00:01:50,650
The European Central Bank faces tough scrutiny over French fiscal deficits and whether sovereign debt threatens eurozone stability.

17
00:01:50,650 --> 00:01:56,741
Meanwhile, the Bank of Japan stands as the biggest wild card for global capital flows.

18
00:01:56,741 --> 00:02:04,081
Any surprise signal from Tokyo will jolt the yen, Japanese bond yields, and global yen carry trades.

19
00:02:04,081 --> 00:02:13,089
Thursday, October 29 delivers the week's macro climax with third-quarter US GDP and September PCE inflation dropping together.

20
00:02:13,089 --> 00:02:19,425
Institutional desks are mapping four distinct market pricing scenarios around this dual data release.

21
00:02:19,425 --> 00:02:27,292
Strong GDP paired with hot PCE signals economic overheating and stubborn inflation, keeping rate hikes on the table.

22
00:02:27,292 --> 00:02:35,442
That outcome spikes Treasury yields, crushes high-multiple tech stocks, and drives defensive rotation into value and energy.

23
00:02:35,442 --> 00:02:43,996
Resilient GDP alongside cooling PCE creates a Goldilocks soft landing, giving tech stocks room for valuation recovery.

24
00:02:43,996 --> 00:02:52,550
Weak GDP combined with sticky PCE traps markets in stagflation, spiking volatility as stocks and bonds both struggle.

25
00:02:52,550 --> 00:03:00,871
Finally, cooling GDP and falling PCE revive easing hopes, boosting bonds while equities weigh slower earnings growth.

26
00:03:00,871 --> 00:03:08,775
Traders will also track Dallas and Richmond Fed manufacturing, durable goods, new home sales, and trade balances.

27
00:03:08,775 --> 00:03:16,349
Inventories, the Employment Cost Index, PMI, and consumer confidence round out the American economic picture.

28
00:03:16,349 --> 00:03:26,226
Across the Atlantic, German business climate, preliminary CPI readings, and eurozone third-quarter GDP will test European economic weakness.

29
00:03:26,226 --> 00:03:32,402
Middle East geopolitical landmines add a volatile wildcard to crude oil and inflation pricing.

30
00:03:32,402 --> 00:03:40,307
Houthi forces repeatedly struck Saudi energy facilities and airports, while two supertankers hit mines near the Hormuz Strait.

31
00:03:40,307 --> 00:03:46,937
Washington and Tehran are trading sharp warnings over potential military strikes and regional oil supplies.

32
00:03:46,937 --> 00:03:54,547
Donald Trump floated a limited Epic Fury strike or seizing Iranian oil, weighing immediate action against slow attrition.

33
00:03:54,547 --> 00:04:00,675
Tehran vowed not to yield to new strikes while pointing directly to American midterm election pressures.

34
00:04:00,675 --> 00:04:08,959
Tuesday's Israeli parliamentary election introduces immediate political uncertainty around coalition building and regional security policy.

35
00:04:08,959 --> 00:04:15,197
Any sudden military escalation around the vote would trigger an immediate spike in global crude prices.

36
00:04:15,197 --> 00:04:24,351
Still, geopolitical oil rallies fade quickly unless backed by global demand, OPEC Plus supply policy, and inventory fundamentals.

37
00:04:24,488 --> 00:04:31,888
Macro liquidity pressures are flowing directly into technology capital expenditure cycles and broader industry rotation.

38
00:04:31,838 --> 00:04:39,437
Following Meta's recent earnings warning, investors fear cloud giants will ramp up AI spending at the expense of margins.

39
00:04:39,437 --> 00:04:47,022
Wall Street demands proof that massive AI infrastructure spending generates fresh revenue without draining free cash flow.

40
00:04:47,022 --> 00:04:55,638
Beyond tech, a five-day corporate earnings gauntlet will test defensive positioning, consumer resilience, and industrial supply chains.

41
00:04:55,638 --> 00:05:04,191
Today, Verizon opens the slate, testing defensive appetite through wireless demand, subscriber churn, and free cash flow.

42
00:05:04,191 --> 00:05:13,309
On Tuesday, Boeing tests global aviation supply chains, aircraft deliveries, order backlogs, and industrial cash generation.

43
00:05:13,309 --> 00:05:20,086
Visa reports the same day, measuring everyday consumer spending power and cross-border commercial momentum.

44
00:05:20,086 --> 00:05:27,071
On Thursday morning, Eli Lilly and Merck highlight pharmaceutical pipelines against the headwind of high interest rates.

45
00:05:27,071 --> 00:05:34,228
Friday closes with traditional energy titans ExxonMobil, Chevron, and B P reporting quarterly results.

46
00:05:34,228 --> 00:05:43,248
Investors will verify whether Middle East oil spikes actually translate into upstream profits, cash flow, buybacks, and dividends.

47
00:05:43,248 --> 00:05:50,184
If rising production costs offset higher crude prices, energy stocks will face sharp internal divergence.

48
00:05:50,184 --> 00:05:57,010
At the micro level, four pivotal corporate battles define single-stock positioning and risk management this week.

49
00:05:57,010 --> 00:06:04,265
Coca-Cola reports Tuesday after hitting fresh record highs and crossing the four-hundred-billion-dollar market cap milestone.

50
00:06:04,265 --> 00:06:12,427
Remarkably, the one-hundred-four-year-old giant is seeing unit volume growth outpace price hikes, fueled by zero-sugar adoption.

51
00:06:12,427 --> 00:06:20,503
Kentucky Fried Chicken also ended its decades-long Pepsi contract to switch to Coca-Cola, reshaping restaurant channel dominance.

52
00:06:20,503 --> 00:06:28,444
Holding above the four-hundred-billion-dollar valuation mark keeps institutional momentum firmly intact for defensive consumer staples.

53
00:06:28,444 --> 00:06:35,613
Wednesday after the bell brings peak volatility as Microsoft and Google report alongside the Federal Reserve decision.

54
00:06:35,613 --> 00:06:44,253
For Microsoft, institutional positioning hinges on Azure cloud growth rates, AI order conversion, and capital expenditure discipline.

55
00:06:44,253 --> 00:06:52,660
For Google, traders are scrutinizing core search advertising resilience against generative AI disruption and cloud return on investment.

56
00:06:52,660 --> 00:07:00,405
Any free cash flow compression from runaway Capex will trigger institutional de-risking across the Nasdaq one hundred benchmark.

57
00:07:00,405 --> 00:07:07,733
Finally, Intel reports Thursday after the close to validate the broader semiconductor and AI hardware cycle.

58
00:07:07,733 --> 00:07:15,454
Markets will audit Intel's data center recovery, AI progress, foundry turnaround, and capital spending plans.

59
00:07:15,454 --> 00:07:23,248
Alongside memory and compute supply chain trends, Intel's guidance will set the defensive floor for the semiconductor index.

60
00:07:23,248 --> 00:07:27,255
That concludes today's XiaoFang Finance Morning Brief.

61
00:07:27,255 --> 00:07:31,422
Thank you for listening, trade safely, and see you tomorrow.
